# Line endings.
#
# Committed content was already LF throughout — `core.autocrlf=true` on this
# machine was normalising correctly on the way in, so no commit has ever
# carried CRLF churn. What it was *not* doing is keeping the working tree in
# step: 75 of 181 tracked files sat as CRLF on disk against an all-LF index,
# and git announced that on every single command ("LF will be replaced by CRLF
# the next time Git touches it"). Enough noise to hide a real warning.
#
# `eol=lf` is what fixes it, rather than `text=auto` alone. auto only decides
# what the *index* holds; eol pins the checkout too, so disk and index agree
# and the warnings stop having anything to report. It also removes the
# dependence on each clone's core.autocrlf: a machine with it set to false
# would otherwise start committing CRLF the moment it edited a file.
#
# Everything here is deployed to Linux, so LF is the right default. The two
# exceptions below are the files where it genuinely is not.
#
# After changing this file: `git add --renormalize .`, then `git rm -r --cached .`
# + `git reset --hard` on a clean tree to rewrite the working copies.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Named explicitly rather than left to `text=auto`, which decides by sniffing
# content. That heuristic is right nearly always, and "nearly" is what
# produces a file that behaves differently from its neighbours for reasons
# nobody can see.
*.py     text
*.js     text
*.vue    text
*.css    text
*.html   text
*.json   text
*.md     text
*.yml    text
*.yaml   text
*.toml   text
*.ini    text
*.sql    text
*.txt    text
*.svg    text
*.mako   text
*.example text

# Already covered by the eol=lf default above, but stated outright because
# these are the ones where getting it wrong is a production incident rather
# than an annoyance: they are read by Linux at deploy time, and a CRLF on a
# shebang or a process definition makes the interpreter unfindable — a
# container that will not start, reported as a missing file that is plainly
# right there.
*.sh     text eol=lf
Procfile text eol=lf

# The exceptions. cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only batch files, and these two never
# leave Windows — they set up the archived Streamlit app on Sonny's PC.
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.ps1    text eol=crlf

# Never normalise and never diff as text — line-ending translation corrupts
# these outright.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.webp   binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.eot    binary
*.db     binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary

# The curriculum workbooks are tracked (see research_and_development/). They
# are zip archives; a byte of CRLF translation makes them unopenable.
*.xlsx   binary
*.xls    binary
